## Goal
Perform principal component analysis (PCA) on normalized gene counts to visualize sample relationships in PC1 × PC2 space, coloured by experimental condition. This reveals sample clustering patterns and the magnitude of the condition effect on overall transcriptome variation.

## Inputs
Artifact types resolve through each sub-stage's OUTPUTS.tsv at execution time; the paths
below are what they resolve to today.

| Artifact type | Resolved from | Path |
|---|---|---|
| `counts_gene` | 01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper | 02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper/run/results/star_salmon/salmon.merged.gene_counts_length_scaled.tsv |
| `design` | 01_prepare_samplesheets | 01_samplesheets/rnaseq_bulk_design.csv |

## Method

1. Load gene counts from the count matrix and sample metadata from the design file. Filter out genes with fewer than 1 count per million (CPM) in fewer than 2 samples to remove low-abundance noise.
2. Apply transcripts-per-million (TPM) normalization to the filtered count matrix using the edgeR-style library size calculation.
3. Log2-transform the TPM values (log2(TPM + 1)) to stabilize variance.
4. Standardize each gene to mean 0, variance 1.
5. Perform PCA on the standardized matrix using scikit-learn's PCA, retaining all components.
6. Generate a scatter plot of PC1 vs PC2, sized by 600×500 pixels, with each point representing a sample, coloured by condition (untreated: blue, treated: red). Include condition legend and PC1/PC2 variance explained in axis labels.
7. Export the plot as a high-resolution PNG file (300 dpi).
8. Output a summary table with sample IDs, condition, PC1, and PC2 coordinates for reference.

Environment: gars-bio (conda). All steps run in a single Python script using pandas, scikit-learn, and matplotlib.

